<div class="three-tool-facts"><div><span>Daily checklist</span><strong>Five Fard prayers</strong></div><div><span>History</span><strong>Up to 90 days</strong></div><div><span>Storage</span><strong>Your browser only</strong></div></div>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="salah-deep-five"><h3>What are the five daily Salah prayers?</h3><p>Fajr, Dhuhr, Asr, Maghrib and Isha are the five daily obligatory prayers. <a href="https://quran.com/4/103">Quran 4:103</a> describes prayer as appointed for believers at specified times. The tracker has five independently selectable checkboxes per recorded civil day; sunrise and optional Sunnah are not additional Fard slots. Checking one off records your own action and does not determine religious validity or acceptance.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="salah-deep-use"><h3>How to use the Salah tracker</h3><ol><li>Open the calculator. Today's date appears using your device's local civil date.</li><li>Tick a prayer after you personally choose to record its completion.</li><li>To review a previous day, choose a date within the 90-day window and adjust the boxes.</li><li>Select 7, 30 or 90 days to see counts of recorded days and unrecorded days separately.</li><li>Export a CSV for recorded dates, or create a JSON backup before replacing devices or clearing storage.</li><li>To restore, choose a valid backup and confirm replacement; current data is not overwritten by invalid files.</li></ol></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="salah-deep-unknown"><h3>Why an unrecorded date does not mean a missed Salah</h3><p>Personal recordkeeping is incomplete by nature. A day with no entry means only that the user did not record checkmarks; it cannot tell if worship took place. Even a recorded day with fewer than five ticks may reflect incomplete logging. This tool does <strong>not</strong> calculate religiously missed Salah or Qada debt from these blanks. That distinction matters when planning religious obligations.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="salah-deep-privacy"><h3>Where is my prayer history stored and who can see it?</h3><p>The tracker uses the browser's local storage key for existing Salah history. It does not sync to sunnah.tools servers or require registration. Someone with access to the same browser profile or device may still view records. Clearing storage, using private browsing or changing devices can remove the history; export the JSON backup if you want to preserve it.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="salah-deep-example"><h3>Worked example: recorded versus unrecorded days</h3><div class="three-tool-example"><p>In a seven-day window, suppose you recorded prayer checkmarks on four days: five ticks, four ticks, five ticks and three ticks. The summary shows <strong>four recorded days</strong>, <strong>three unrecorded days (unknown)</strong>, and <strong>17 personal prayer ticks</strong> among twenty possible slots for those recorded dates.</p><p>It would be misleading to call the remaining eighteen boxes 'missed'. The three entirely unrecorded days and any incomplete checked days may not reflect actual prayer. The tracker displays observational counts, not religious rulings.</p></div></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="salah-deep-export"><h3>How the CSV and JSON backup work</h3><p>CSV lists dates for which an explicit record exists and marks the prayers you ticked. The JSON backup uses a validated, versioned schema compatible with the existing Salah Tracker. Restore checks dates, permitted prayer names and duplicate values and asks before replacing local history. It keeps at most the latest 90 days so your file does not grow indefinitely. There is no cloud backup or remote recovery service.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="salah-deep-travel"><h3>Does the calendar reset when my timezone changes?</h3><p>The current day is based on the user's device-local civil date, refreshed periodically. Timezone changes or travelling can alter which civil date is currently selected; verify your travel-day entries before marking. An app date is not a substitute for local astronomical prayer starts. For prayer times use <a href="/tools/prayer-times/">Prayer Times</a>.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="salah-deep-different"><h3>Salah tracking versus Qada, Witr and Sunnah tracking</h3><p>A daily self-tracker records checkmarks. <em>Qada</em> concerns making up prayers determined to have been missed under a religious ruling, which can depend on why and when a prayer was omitted. Witr and recommended Sunnah prayers have school-specific details. Use a separate <a href="/tools/qada-prayer-calculator/">Qada plan</a> only with qualified guidance. Do not mechanically import missing checkmarks as Qada obligations.</p></section>
